w()
th = GE["thermal"]["variants"]["released|as_coded"]
w(f"**Reading.** On the SI's own test split, {100 * (1 - GE['thermal']['n_clean'] / GE['thermal']['n_test']):.0f}% (thermal), {100 * (1 - GE['solvent']['n_clean'] / GE['solvent']['n_test']):.0f}% (solvent) and {100 * (1 - GE['water']['n_clean'] / GE['water']['n_test']):.0f}% (water) of the test MOFs have a same-group MOF in training, and the metrics differ sharply between the two halves: for the released thermal network R2 is {th['leaky']['r2']:.3f} on the leaky half and {th['clean']['r2']:.3f} on the group-clean half. The solvent and water 'clean' metrics are also well below the leaky ones (table). The SI's headline numbers are therefore, on this evidence, inflated by same-family neighbours between training and test, in line with Phase 2's random-vs-group result. The clean subsets are small (thermal {GE['thermal']['n_clean']}, solvent {GE['solvent']['n_clean']}, water {GE['water']['n_clean']} MOFs), so the intervals are wide, and the clean/leaky halves also differ in other ways (group size, family composition), so this is an association, not a controlled comparison.")
