w("## 5. Group-vs-random gaps as ranges over many splits")
w()
w("Split seed s gives one random **group** split (`random_group_assign`: all groups shuffled, cut at 60/20/20 by MOF count) and one MOF-level random split (`random_assign`), both over the union of the three tables; the same model family is trained on each (60%) and scored on the test part (about 20%). **Gap = random - group, paired by split seed** (for MAE: group - random). Cells: mean [2.5%, 97.5% percentile over splits] (min-max). The earlier single-split gaps (`phase2.md` section 2) are single draws from these distributions.")
w()
def table(df, name, n):
    w(f"### {name} (N = {n} splits)")
    w()
    w("| stage | metric | random split | group split | gap | share of splits with gap > 0 |")
    w("|---|---|---|---|---|---|")
    for st in ("thermal", "solvent", "water"):
        for k in [c for c in df.columns if c not in ("split_seed", "mode", "stage", "n_test", "n_train") and df[df.stage == st][c].notna().any()]:
            a = df[(df.stage == st) & (df["mode"] == "random")].set_index("split_seed")[k]; b = df[(df.stage == st) & (df["mode"] == "group")].set_index("split_seed")[k]
            g = (b - a) if k == "mae_degC" else (a - b)
            fm = lambda x: f"{x.mean():.3f} [{x.quantile(.025):.3f}, {x.quantile(.975):.3f}] ({x.min():.3f}-{x.max():.3f})"
            w(f"| {st} | {k}{' (gap = group - random)' if k == 'mae_degC' else ''} | {fm(a)} | {fm(b)} | {fm(g)} | {int((g > 0).sum())}/{len(g)} |")
    w()
table(RF, "Random forest, all three stages", RF.split_seed.nunique())
table(NN, "Released networks (fixed restore) for thermal and solvent, released forest for water", NN.split_seed.nunique())
tn = RF[(RF.stage == "water")].pivot(index="split_seed", columns="mode", values="auc_stable"); 
w("**Reading.** The gap is positive in nearly every split for every stage and model family (last column), so the direction is robust. Its size varies a lot from split to split: compare the percentile ranges with the single-split gaps quoted before. Group-aware metrics also have a wide range (the group split with its large indivisible groups gives a different test set every time), so a single split gives neither a reliable gap nor a reliable absolute level. The numbers above replace the single-split gaps as the summary of the group-vs-random effect; `figures/fig1_group_vs_random` still shows the single seed-0 split (its caption says so) and is not redrawn here.")
